[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is complete. Every VLAN 5/6 host reaches its gateway, the startup config is clean, and both trunk subinterfaces are up and forwarding. This is a false-premise ticket.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that VLAN 5 (10.5.0.0/24) and VLAN 6 (10.6.0.0/24) hosts on switch4 lost their uplink to the multilayer switch gateway after maintenance — does not exist in the live lab. Direct probes across all five affected end-hosts (pc1, pc2, dc_pc5 on VLAN 5; pc3, pc4 on VLAN 6) show 0% packet loss to their respective gateways, 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254, both of which are SVI addresses hosted on sshswitch's br_v5 and br_v6 bridge instances.

The trunk between switch4 (eth0) and sshswitch (eth0) is correctly configured on both sides: switch4 creates 802.1Q subinterfaces eth0.5 (VLAN ID 5) and eth0.6 (VLAN ID 6), slaves them into per-VLAN bridges br_vlan5 and br_vlan6, and the running state exactly matches the startup script. On sshswitch the mirror subinterfaces eth0.5/eth0.6 are active members of br_v5/br_v6. Both subinterfaces show state UP and the bridge forwarding state is "forwarding" on both devices. No VLAN has been pruned, removed, or misconfigured on the switch4 trunk.

The root cause is a false premise: the maintenance team's belief that a VLAN was dropped from the trunk is incorrect — the trunk has always carried VLANs 5 and 6, the gateways are reachable, and no corrective action is needed.
=== END ===